{
  // The deployed game: one Worker serving the built static assets and the
  // out-of-band reporting endpoints beside them.
  //
  // This file is committed on purpose. Bindings declared here come into
  // existence on deploy, which is the only durable home for them — a binding
  // added in the dashboard is clobbered by the next deploy from a config that
  // does not declare it (#114, Step 4).
  //
  // Secrets are the opposite: they are set on the Worker with
  // `wrangler secret put` and must never appear here, because this file is in
  // git. Nothing below is a credential.
  //
  // The names are settled in #114 and are not ours to improve.
  //
  // Deployed by `npm run deploy` from the repo root, which builds the index and
  // the bundle first and then runs `wrangler deploy` here — one command, one
  // atomic version carrying the Worker, the bundle and the index together. See
  // docs/deploy.md for that loop and for how to roll a bad one back.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "bramble-bee",
  "main": "worker/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-05",

  // Served from the unlisted `bramble-bee.kenna-dev.workers.dev`. Declared
  // rather than left to default, because it is the whole public surface of the
  // playtest: #113 rules out a custom domain, so this is the address that gets
  // sent to friends.
  "workers_dev": true,

  // The built bundle, with the content-addressed Rhyme Index alongside it:
  // `npm run build` writes the shell here and copies in the one hashed artifact
  // and its manifest, and nothing else from `dist-data` (see indexAssetPlugin).
  // `_headers` lands here too, and Workers reads it from the root of this
  // directory — which is how the cache directives #117 settled on are declared
  // by the deployment rather than inherited from host defaults.
  //
  // Assets are matched before the Worker runs, so loading the game never enters
  // `worker/index.ts`.
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./dist",
    "binding": "ASSETS"
  },

  // Where a should-have-counted Appeal is written, one object per Appeal (#119).
  // A binding, not an access key: the Worker is handed the bucket itself, so
  // there is no credential anywhere on the write path.
  "r2_buckets": [
    {
      "binding": "APPEAL_QUEUE",
      "bucket_name": "rhyme-bee-flags"
    }
  ],

  // The tracker a general note is filed on (#118). A plaintext var, not a
  // secret: which repository this game reports to is not a confidence, and
  // naming it here keeps the deployment fact out of the source.
  //
  // The credential that files it is deliberately absent from this file. The
  // fine-grained token lives as the Worker secret `GITHUB_ISSUE_TOKEN`, set
  // with `wrangler secret put GITHUB_ISSUE_TOKEN --name bramble-bee` and
  // referenced by name only (#114, Step 7) — this file is committed.
  "vars": {
    "ISSUE_REPO": "jkenna-Colgate/rhyme-bee"
  },

  // The write endpoints' budgets (#213). #114 Step 9 decided against a limit
  // for the playtest and said to revisit it if the URL leaked; publishing this
  // repository is that leak, and it fired on both premises at once — the
  // hostname is committed here and in the Vite config, and the tracker a note
  // is filed on became public at the same moment. So this is the revisit, and
  // the binding is here rather than in the dashboard for the reason at the top
  // of this file.
  //
  // Two limiters rather than one shared budget: a junk Appeal costs an R2
  // object, a junk note files an issue under the maintainer's token, and one
  // budget would let either abuse starve the other.
  //
  // `namespace_id` is any positive integer unique within the Cloudflare account,
  // and is what the counters are actually filed under — two bindings sharing one
  // would share one budget, which is the thing the paragraph above rules out. It
  // is not a name and is not shown anywhere; these two are #213's number, so a
  // later pair can be read off its own ticket and not collide by accident.
  //
  // `period` accepts only 10 or 60, and counting is per Cloudflare location, so
  // the real global ceiling is the number below times however many locations a
  // caller reaches. The numbers are therefore chosen to sit well above one
  // player and well below anything worth scripting, not to meter exactly: an
  // Appeal is one tap on one rejection and the button then reads "sent", so 20
  // a minute is far more than a Session can produce; a note is typed prose, and
  // five a minute is already faster than anyone writes.
  //
  // Only players are counted here. The Editor's Pass posts to the Appeal path
  // too, one request per disagreement (#163), and does so in far heavier bursts
  // than any Session — but it runs against the Vite dev server, where that path
  // is `supplementPlugin` and this binding does not exist. `editor.html` is not
  // a build input, so the Editor's Pass never reaches this Worker at all.
  //
  // Requests over the budget are refused in `worker/http.ts`, before the body
  // is read. Serving the game is untouched either way — assets are matched
  // before this Worker runs at all.
  "ratelimits": [
    {
      "name": "APPEAL_LIMITER",
      "namespace_id": "2131",
      "simple": { "limit": 20, "period": 60 }
    },
    {
      "name": "FEEDBACK_LIMITER",
      "namespace_id": "2132",
      "simple": { "limit": 5, "period": 60 }
    }
  ]
}
